@startuml
title logGO - Push Ingestion (three protocols, one path in)

actor "REST client" as RESTClient
actor "WebSocket client" as WSClient
actor "gRPC client" as GRPCClient
participant "handleIngestREST" as REST
participant "handleIngestWS" as WS
participant "ingestServiceHandler\n(gRPC/Connect)" as GRPC
participant "ingest()" as Shared
participant Manager as "logGO.Manager"

== Protocol 1: REST — one request per entry ==
RESTClient -> REST: POST /ingest\n{"source","level","message","fields","timestamp"?}
activate REST
REST -> Shared: ingest(manager, payload)
REST --> RESTClient: 202 Accepted
deactivate REST

== Protocol 2: WebSocket — one connection, many entries ==
WSClient -> WS: GET /ws/ingest (upgrade)
activate WS
loop for each entry, same JSON shape as REST
  WSClient -> WS: text frame: {"source","level","message",...}
  WS -> Shared: ingest(manager, payload)
  note right: malformed JSON is dropped,\nnot a connection close — one bad\nline shouldn't end a long-lived stream
end
deactivate WS

== Protocol 3: gRPC — schema'd, real gRPC wire format over h2c ==
GRPCClient -> GRPC: IngestService.Ingest(IngestRequest{source, entry})
activate GRPC
GRPC -> GRPC: decode entry.fields (each a JSON-encoded\nstring value) back into map[string]any
GRPC -> Shared: ingest(manager, payload)\n(timestamp: entry.timestamp_unix_nano if set, else now)
GRPC --> GRPCClient: IngestResponse{}
deactivate GRPC

== Shared tail: all three protocols land here ==
activate Shared
Shared -> Shared: fields["source"] = payload.Source\n(same tag a pulled entry gets — see 04, 05)
Shared -> Manager: WriteEntry(NewEntry(ts, level, message, fields))
note right: WriteEntry, not WriteLog — preserves\nthe entry's own timestamp (defaults to\ntime.Now() only if the client omitted one),\nsince a push may be relaying something that\nalready happened.
deactivate Shared

note over RESTClient, GRPCClient
  cmd/demo-rest-client, cmd/demo-ws-client, and
  cmd/demo-grpc-client are one complete, working
  example of each — see scripts/demo-push.sh to
  run all three against a live server at once.
  Registering a push source via the UI (or
  POST /sources with kind="push") is optional:
  it just reserves a friendly name/ID and shows
  a connection snippet — pushing works with any
  "source" string, registered or not.
end note

@enduml
